netfilter: nft_rt: bound TCP MSS by the ingress link MTU

A forwarded reply can have a different reverse policy route from the
unmarked lookup in get_tcpmss(). With a 1400-byte USB WAN selected by
mwan3 and a 1500-byte main default, the reply's LAN destination and the
reverse lookup both report 1500, leaving its advertised MSS too large.
Copying skb->mark is insufficient: mwan3 can replace it with its default
mark for a connected LAN destination.

Also bound MSS by the actual ingress link. Keep the existing forward and
reverse route limits, including any smaller path MTU. Evaluations without
an ingress device retain their existing behavior. Forwarded postrouting
packets can retain an ingress device and receive the additional limit too.
Use the device supplied by the hook, not skb->dev, which may already refer
to an output device.

--- a/net/netfilter/nft_rt.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nft_rt.c
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@
 {
 	u32 minlen = sizeof(struct ipv6hdr), mtu = dst_mtu(skbdst);
 	const struct sk_buff *skb = pkt->skb;
+	const struct net_device *in = nft_in(pkt);
 	struct dst_entry *dst = NULL;
 	struct flowi fl;
 
@@ -44,6 +45,10 @@
 		dst_release(dst);
 	}
 
+	/* The unmarked reverse lookup may use a different uplink. */
+	if (in)
+		mtu = min_t(u32, mtu, READ_ONCE(in->mtu));
+
 	if (mtu <= minlen || mtu > 0xffff)
 		return TCP_MSS_DEFAULT;
 
